### Reproducer
godbolt (C, with execution): https://godbolt.org/z/17WPWGz8Y
godbolt (IR, llc): https://godbolt.org/z/hhWhbfPra
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define N ((1ul << 29) + 2)

int main(void) {
  unsigned char *g = calloc(N + 1, 1);
  if (!g)
    return 2;
  g[3] = 7; /* g[0..1] == g[1..2] == {0,0}; g[2] (0) != g[3] (7) */
  printf("%d\n", memcmp(g, g + 1, N) != 0);
  free(g);
  return 0;
}
```
`clang -O2` prints `0`; `clang -O0` and GCC print `1`. The two compared ranges differ at offset 2 (`g[2] = 0`, `g[3] = 7`), so `memcmp(...) != 0` is 1. With `-O2` the call becomes `bcmp`, and codegen compares only the first two bytes (x86-64 shown; AArch64 emits the same two `ldrh` + `cmp`). `-O1` is affected too.
```llvm
target triple = "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"

declare i32 @memcmp(ptr, ptr, i64)

; 2^29 + 2 bytes: only the first 2 bytes are compared
define i1 @test(ptr %p, ptr %q) {
  %c = call i32 @memcmp(ptr %p, ptr %q, i64 536870914)
  %ne = icmp ne i32 %c, 0
  ret i1 %ne
}

; 2^29 + 1 bytes: libcall, as expected
define i1 @clean(ptr %p, ptr %q) {
  %c = call i32 @memcmp(ptr %p, ptr %q, i64 536870913)
  %ne = icmp ne i32 %c, 0
  ret i1 %ne
}
```
```console
$ llc -O2 -mtriple=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -x86-asm-syntax=intel repro.ll
test:
        movzx   eax, word ptr [rdi]
        cmp     ax, word ptr [rsi]
        setne   al
        ret
clean:
        ...
        mov     edx, 536870913
        call    memcmp@PLT
```
`SelectionDAGBuilder::visitMemCmpBCmpCall` computes the bit count in 32 bits:
```cpp
unsigned NumBitsToCompare = CSize->getZExtValue() * 8;
switch (NumBitsToCompare) {
case 16: LoadVT = MVT::i16; break;
case 32: LoadVT = MVT::i32; break;
...
```
For 536870914 = 2^29 + 2 bytes, the product truncates to 16, so the call is lowered to a single `i16` load-and-compare. Any constant size `n >= 2^29` with `n mod 2^29` in {2, 4} is affected on every target, and {8, 16, 32} wherever the target's `hasFastEqualityCompare` accepts that width (x86-64: 8 and 16 at baseline, 32 with AVX), starting at an ordinary 512 MiB size. The initial SelectionDAG already has the two `i16` loads, so this is the builder and not a DAG combine. The line came from f01a1dad7f36 ("[x86] use VPMOVMSK to replace memcmp libcalls for 32-byte equality", 2017), which replaced a switch on the byte count with a switch on this bit count. Switching on the byte count again (or using a `uint64_t` bit count) would fix it.

#54786 is a similar `Len * 8` overflow in `optimizeMemCmpConstantSize` (SimplifyLibCalls.cpp, InstCombine), but it wraps only near 2^61 and is a different code path. It is not involved here.

Disclaimer: This test case was generated by a fuzzer, and the report was written with LLM assistance.
